Align by DERIVING from the pointer, not by rebuilding one from its address.

Four sites in the allocator align a pointer by masking through uintptr_t:

    char *buf_aligned = (char *)(((uintptr_t)buf + 7) & (uintptr_t)~7);

On a target whose pointers carry out-of-band metadata this is not a rounding
operation, it is a discard: the cast to uintptr_t keeps the address and drops
everything else, and the pointer built back from it has no metadata to carry.
The first write through it raises "unexpected operand type".

Measured rather than argued. A staged bring-up put the fault exactly here:
os_malloc from the port's own arena returns, the gp-captable carve of file-scope
globals returns, and wasm_runtime_memory_init -- which calls gc_init_with_pool,
which is line 63 below -- does not.

The fix keeps the arithmetic and changes what it is applied to: compute the same
aligned address, then move the ORIGINAL pointer by the difference. Same value,
same alignment, provenance preserved, and it compiles to identical code on a
target where a pointer is just an address.

This is the same defect and the same fix as MicroPython's
0003-gc-align-down-without-losing-the-pointer, in a different allocator. Two
runtimes independently written, one pattern.

diff --git i/core/shared/mem-alloc/ems/ems_alloc.c w/core/shared/mem-alloc/ems/ems_alloc.c
index 0a3e086..e21c87d 100644
--- i/core/shared/mem-alloc/ems/ems_alloc.c
+++ w/core/shared/mem-alloc/ems/ems_alloc.c
@@ -710,7 +710,9 @@ gc_alloc_vo_aligned_internal(void *vheap, gc_size_t size, gc_size_t alignment,
     aligned_addr =
         (((uintptr_t)base_obj + ALIGNED_ALLOC_METADATA_SIZE + alignment - 1)
          & ~(uintptr_t)(alignment - 1));
-    ret = (gc_object_t)aligned_addr;
+    /* base_obj + delta, not (gc_object_t)aligned_addr: the second discards the
+       pointer and keeps only its address. */
+    ret = (gc_object_t)(base_obj + (aligned_addr - (uintptr_t)base_obj));
 
     /* Verify we have enough space */
     bh_assert((gc_uint8 *)ret + size + OBJ_SUFFIX_SIZE
diff --git i/core/shared/mem-alloc/ems/ems_kfc.c w/core/shared/mem-alloc/ems/ems_kfc.c
index e761cb6..c1a040b 100644
--- i/core/shared/mem-alloc/ems/ems_kfc.c
+++ w/core/shared/mem-alloc/ems/ems_kfc.c
@@ -60,7 +60,12 @@ gc_handle_t
 gc_init_with_pool(char *buf, gc_size_t buf_size)
 {
     char *buf_end = buf + buf_size;
-    char *buf_aligned = (char *)(((uintptr_t)buf + 7) & (uintptr_t)~7);
+    /* Derive from buf, do not rebuild from its address: masking through
+       uintptr_t yields a plain integer, and the pointer made from it carries no
+       metadata on a target where pointers have some. Same value, kept
+       provenance. */
+    char *buf_aligned =
+        buf + (((uintptr_t)buf + 7) & (uintptr_t)~7) - (uintptr_t)buf;
     char *base_addr = buf_aligned + sizeof(gc_heap_t);
     gc_heap_t *heap = (gc_heap_t *)buf_aligned;
     gc_size_t heap_max_size;
@@ -71,8 +76,9 @@ gc_init_with_pool(char *buf, gc_size_t buf_size)
         return NULL;
     }
 
-    base_addr =
-        (char *)(((uintptr_t)base_addr + 7) & (uintptr_t)~7) + GC_HEAD_PADDING;
+    base_addr = base_addr
+                + (((uintptr_t)base_addr + 7) & (uintptr_t)~7) - (uintptr_t)base_addr
+                + GC_HEAD_PADDING;
     heap_max_size = (uint32)(buf_end - base_addr) & (uint32)~7;
 
 #if WASM_ENABLE_MEMORY_TRACING != 0
